:"LAC","distance":1.063,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"PIT","distance":1.08,"shared":21,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"HOU","distance":1.098,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Bobby Slowik"}],"defense":[{"season":2024,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.7639,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Jeff Ulbrich"},{"season":2021,"team":"DET","distance":0.8329,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Aaron Glenn"},{"season":2018,"team":"NYG","distance":0.8436,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"PIT","distance":0.9115,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"DET","distance":0.9491,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Aaron Glenn"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Ravens went 13-4 in 2023 and outscored opponents 483 to 280. Both sides of the ball were strong, with the offense 5th in EPA per play and the defense 2nd in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the lowest pass rate and the 2nd lowest two-high safety rate in the league.","offense":"The Ravens had one of the most efficient offenses in the league, ranking in the top eight in all five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 3rd highest 21 personnel usage and the 3rd fewest tight ends on the field in the league. Lamar Jackson ran the passing game, with Zay Flowers leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Ravens had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 7th or better in four of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was EPA allowed per play, where it ranked 2nd, and its weakest was pressure rate, where it ranked 18th. Nnamdi Madubuike led the team with 13 sacks, and Jadeveon Clowney added 9.5 while playing only 57% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd lowest two-high safety rate in the league.","special":"The Ravens were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 18% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 86% of field goals, which was about average. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way.","players":"Lamar Jackson played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,678 yards. Zay Flowers was the clear top target with 23% of the looks and 858 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Gus Edwards and Justice Hill. On defense, Nnamdi Madubuike led the team with 13 sacks and Geno Stone picked off 7 passes.","coaching":"Todd Monken called the offense and Mike Macdonald called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Bears and the 2022 49ers under Kyle Shanahan. Their defense looked most like the 2024 Jets under Jeff Ulbrich and the 2021 Lions under Aaron Glenn.","trends":"The offense went from 15th in EPA per play in 2022 to 5th in 2023, a big step forward. The defense moved from 15th in EPA allowed per play in 2022 to 2nd in 2023, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was number of tight ends on the field, which went from 1.65 to 1.06.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2023. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}